INT. KINGSLEY FAMILY MINIVAN - ROAD-TRIP DETOUR - DAY

A battered silver minivan crawls along a gravel road through sunflower fields. Cracker crumbs on the seats. A cooler wedged between the front seats. Windows down.

At the wheel, GLORIA KINGSLEY (40s, sunglasses on her head, squinting at a paper map). Beside her, her husband ARTHUR KINGSLEY (40s, sunburnt nose, a sandwich in one hand). In the back, their daughter PRUDENCE "PRU" KINGSLEY (11, library book, headphones around her neck, one foot on the seat in front).

GLORIA
The sign said Millbrook was four miles.

ARTHUR
That was six miles ago.

PRU
It said four, then it said nine, then it said Millbrook closed.

GLORIA
It did not say closed.

PRU
It said "CLOSED FOR SEASON." I read it.

ARTHUR
That's a different sign. That was the minigolf.

PRU
There's minigolf in the middle of nowhere?

ARTHUR
There's always minigolf.

She puts down the map and shades her eyes. A cloud of dust rolls up behind them. The minivan coughs. The dashboard light flickers.

GLORIA
(to herself)
Don't you dare.

ARTHUR
What is that noise?

GLORIA
Nothing is making a noise.

PRU
It's the van.

GLORIA
It's not the van.

The engine sputters again and dies. The van coasts a few yards, rolls to a stop beside a rusted mailbox leaning in the weeds.

Silence. Cicadas. A sunflower nods in the wind.

ARTHUR
(quietly)
So.

GLORIA
Don't say it.

ARTHUR
I wasn't going to say it.

PRU
You were.

GLORIA
He was.

ARTHUR
I was going to say "Great spot for a picnic."

Pru snorts. Gloria turns the key. Click. Click. Nothing.

GLORIA
We have water. We have the cooler. We have a map.

PRU
We have a map that said Millbrook was four miles.

GLORIA
Pru.

PRU
I'm just saying.

Arthur opens his door and steps out, stretching. He kicks a tire with the air of a man who knows it will do nothing.

ARTHUR
I'm going to walk to that farmhouse.

GLORIA
Take your hat.

ARTHUR
I'm not wearing a hat.

GLORIA
Take mine.

He sighs, ducks back in, takes her straw hat from the dashboard, and plops it on his head. Pru laughs out loud.

PRU
You look like a scarecrow.

ARTHUR
That's the idea. If anyone sees me, they'll be afraid and give me a lift.

PRU
Or they'll shoot you.

GLORIA
Pru!

ARTHUR
She has a point.

He starts down the gravel road. Gloria climbs out, shades her eyes, and watches him go. Pru slides across the seat, props her chin on the window.

PRU
Mom?

GLORIA
Yes?

PRU
This is the best trip we've ever had.

GLORIA
We're stranded in a cornfield.

PRU
Sunflowers.

GLORIA
(smiling)
Sunflowers.

A low growl rolls over the horizon. Thunder. They both look up. A line of dark clouds is climbing the sky, fast.

GLORIA (CONT'D)
(muttering)
Of course.

PRU
Should we get Dad?

GLORIA
He'll find us.

PRU
Mom, I'm serious.

GLORIA
(calling)
Arthur!

Far down the road, a small straw-hatted figure turns. Gloria waves her arms. He waves back, cheerful, and keeps walking.

PRU
He doesn't get it.

GLORIA
He never does.

Wind picks up. Sunflowers bow in a long wave across the field. The first fat raindrop splats on the windshield. Gloria and Pru lock eyes.

PRU (CONT'D)
Run?

GLORIA
Run.

They bolt from the minivan, laughing, shrieking, and chase after Arthur through the dust as the sky opens.

ARTHUR
(turning, seeing them)
Oh no.

He starts to run too. The three of them pelt down the road in the downpour, hat flying off, a tiny, soaked, shrieking family under the enormous sky.
